WEBVTT

NOTE English translation of the Vietnamese narration script. Timing is distributed proportionally within each slide segment, since the narration itself is in Vietnamese.

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.033
Hello everyone, this is the revision video for Chapter Two:

2
00:00:04.033 --> 00:00:05.839
Professional communication skills.

3
00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:10.200
This chapter covers four skills you'll use almost every day throughout your career.

4
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.182
The first skill is making a first impression.

5
00:00:15.182 --> 00:00:18.778
Remember the four-times-twenty rule: the first twenty seconds,

6
00:00:18.778 --> 00:00:20.112
the first twenty steps,

7
00:00:20.112 --> 00:00:26.028
the first twenty centimetres of your face — meaning your eyes and smile — and your first twenty words.

8
00:00:26.028 --> 00:00:30.080
A first impression forms almost instantly and is very hard to reverse,

9
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.800
so it must be prepared for, not left to chance.

10
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.253
Along with this come social courtesies.

11
00:00:37.253 --> 00:00:41.201
Greetings: the younger greets the older, staff greet their superiors first.

12
00:00:41.201 --> 00:00:43.480
Handshakes: stand up, look them in the eye,

13
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:49.151
hold for a moderate two to three seconds; the person of higher status, or a woman, offers their hand first.

14
00:00:49.151 --> 00:00:53.178
Business cards: give and receive with both hands, text facing the recipient,

15
00:00:53.178 --> 00:00:57.100
read it before putting it away — don't shove it straight into your pocket.

16
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.440
The second skill is presenting.

17
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.745
Prepare in five steps: analyse your audience, define your objective,

18
00:01:05.745 --> 00:01:08.720
build content around at most three main points,

19
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.645
design slides with little text and more visuals, and rehearse.

20
00:01:12.645 --> 00:01:16.052
A talk's structure is: opening ten to fifteen percent,

21
00:01:16.052 --> 00:01:20.469
body seventy to eighty percent, and conclusion ten to fifteen percent.

22
00:01:20.469 --> 00:01:24.100
Remember this: speak to your audience, not to your slides.

23
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.154
The third skill is listening.

24
00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:32.303
There are five levels: ignoring, pretend listening, selective listening,

25
00:01:32.303 --> 00:01:34.493
attentive listening, and, the highest,

26
00:01:34.493 --> 00:01:39.218
empathic listening — understanding both the emotion and the need behind the words.

27
00:01:39.218 --> 00:01:42.776
Active listening means not interrupting, not rushing to judge,

28
00:01:42.776 --> 00:01:48.400
noting the key points, and paraphrasing to confirm: if I understand correctly, what you mean is...

29
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.123
The fourth skill is communicating by phone.

30
00:01:53.123 --> 00:01:57.432
An outgoing call has four steps: prepare beforehand, open properly by greeting,

31
00:01:57.432 --> 00:02:00.378
identifying yourself and asking permission to proceed,

32
00:02:00.378 --> 00:02:04.687
state your business concisely then summarise the agreement, and close politely.

33
00:02:04.687 --> 00:02:07.361
When answering, pick up within about three rings,

34
00:02:07.361 --> 00:02:10.198
greet the caller and state your organisation's name.

35
00:02:10.198 --> 00:02:12.900
The caller can hear your smile through your voice.

36
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.733
Three things to remember from this chapter.

37
00:02:17.733 --> 00:02:20.731
A first impression is prepared, not a matter of luck.

38
00:02:20.731 --> 00:02:24.125
A good presentation starts with understanding your audience.

39
00:02:24.125 --> 00:02:28.538
And listening together with asking questions is the best sales skill there is.

40
00:02:28.538 --> 00:02:32.724
Go ahead and complete the forty multiple-choice questions for Chapter Two.

41
00:02:32.724 --> 00:02:34.138
See you in Chapter Three.
